# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cador-addattribute-argument-order — Block.AddAttribute swaps InsertionPoint and Tag and ignores Value
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: cad-runtime
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-cador-addattribute-argument-order
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

The SCHME+ ActiveX A1 scenario =creation insertion et mise a jour d'un bloc
attribue= fails under clautolisp/Cador with:

#+begin_example
AddAttribute expects a point (a VARIANT/SAFEARRAY of doubles or a number list),
got "REPERE".
#+end_example

The application call in
=LSP/fonctions-utilitaires/technique/bloc.lsp= follows the AutoCAD ActiveX
signature and passes six arguments after the receiver:

#+begin_src lisp
(vla-AddAttribute bloc
                  hauteur-attribut
                  acAttributeModeNormal
                  prompt-text
                  point-ancrage
                  identifiant-attribut
                  valeur-par-defaut)
#+end_src

For the failing case those values are equivalent to:

#+begin_example
Height         = 1.0
Mode           = acAttributeModeNormal
Prompt         = ""
InsertionPoint = (0.0 0.0 0.0)
Tag            = "REPERE"
Value          = ""
#+end_example

AutoCAD and BricsCAD accept this ordering.  The failure is in Cador's method
adapter, before the ATTDEF entity is created.

Observed in SCHME+ pipeline 86651, job 516228
=(test-cad-activex:clautolisp:linux)= at commit =bf0cd375=.

* Cause

The ActiveX contract is:

#+begin_example
Block.AddAttribute(Height, Mode, Prompt, InsertionPoint, Tag, Value)
#+end_example

But =clautolisp/cador/source/vlax-api.lisp= currently documents and
destructures another signature:

#+begin_src lisp
(defun %block-add-attribute (host collection-kind args)
  "Block.AddAttribute(Height, Mode, Prompt, Tag, InsertionPoint): ..."
  (destructuring-bind
      (&optional height mode prompt tag insertion-point &rest ignore)
      args
    ...
    (ip (%unwrap-com-point insertion-point "AddAttribute"))
    ...))
#+end_src

Given the valid SCHME+ call, Cador therefore binds:

#+begin_example
tag             <- point-ancrage
insertion-point <- "REPERE"
ignore          <- ("")
#+end_example

=%unwrap-com-point= then receives the tag string and emits the observed error.
The sixth =Value= argument is discarded, and the resulting ATTDEF's DXF group
1 is always hard-coded to the empty string:

#+begin_src lisp
(cons 1 "")
#+end_src

* False-green regression test

The existing test
=vlax-block-addattribute-adds-an-attdef-to-the-block= in
=clautolisp/cador/tests/vlax-tests.lisp= passes the arguments in the same wrong
order as the implementation:

#+begin_src lisp
(host-vlax-invoke-method host block "AddAttribute"
  (list 2.5d0 0 "Enter name" "NAME" '(1.0d0 1.0d0 0.0d0)))
#+end_src

It places ="NAME"= where the real API requires =InsertionPoint= and omits the
sixth =Value=.  Consequently the test validates Cador against its own mistaken
contract rather than against AutoCAD/BricsCAD, and cannot catch this defect.

* Fix

Change the adapter to consume all six arguments in vendor order:

#+begin_src lisp
(destructuring-bind
    (&optional height mode prompt insertion-point tag value &rest ignore)
    args
  ...)
#+end_src

Then populate the ATTDEF consistently:

- DXF group 10 from =InsertionPoint=;
- group 40 from =Height=;
- group 3 from =Prompt=;
- group 2 from =Tag=;
- group 1 from =Value=;
- group 70 from =Mode=.

Correct the existing test to use the vendor order and six arguments.  Add
assertions for the insertion point, prompt, tag and default value so that a
future permutation cannot remain green merely because an object was returned.

The public =vla-AddAttribute= and =vlax-invoke-method= paths must both preserve
the argument list unchanged before it reaches this adapter.

* Acceptance criteria

- Cador implements
  =AddAttribute(Height, Mode, Prompt, InsertionPoint, Tag, Value)= in exactly
  that order.
- A non-empty default =Value= is stored in DXF group 1 and visible through the
  corresponding ActiveX property.
- The regression test uses a point, tag and value which are observably
  different and asserts every mapped field.
- A test through the AutoLISP =vla-AddAttribute= wrapper covers the same
  six-argument call, rather than testing only =host-vlax-invoke-method=
  directly.
- The SCHME+ =creation insertion et mise a jour d'un bloc attribue= scenario
  gets past attribute-definition creation and no longer reports that
  ="REPERE"= was supplied as a point.
- Existing =Blocks.Add=, =InsertBlock= and attribute-reference update
  behaviour remains green.

* Related

- =issues/closed/cador-schme-a1-activex-coverage.issue= claimed the A1
  =Block.AddAttribute= surface complete, but its test encoded this wrong
  signature.
- =issues/open/clautolisp-distributed-native-libraries-not-loaded.issue= is
  the independent DWG writer failure exposed by the same SCHME+ A1 job.
